  "strategy": "## Approach and Rationale\n\nThe central idea is to leverage the known optimal structure for sequences with minimal auto-correlation magnitude. Previous works have shown that sequences composed of a small number of distinct values clustered in sub-intervals can effectively minimize \\(\\max(f*f)\\). This approach systematically explores multiple structural templates through cyclic refinement, each with tailored optimization hyperparameters. We focus on candidates generated via:\n\n1. **Repetitive Cluster Optimization** - Where the sequence is divided into \\(k\\) repeated blocks, each with identical values but different positions\n2. **Sub-Interval Structured Optimization** - Where the sequence is divided into \\(k\\) non-overlapping sub-intervals with constant values\n3. **Self-Similar Optimization** - Where peaks are maintained while troughs are strategically reduced\n\nThe key bottleneck in earlier approaches was either local convergence or premature termination due to computational complexity. Here, we delay strict convergence checks by first scanning for promising templates before committing to heavy-large optimization cycles.\n\n## Mathematical Specification\n\nWe represent a sequence \\(a\\) of length \\(n\\) either as:\n\n- **Cluster-based**: \\(a = [x_0, x_1, \\dots, x_{k-1}], [x_0, x_1, \\dots, x_{k-1}], \\dots\\) (periodic)\n- **Sub-interval-based**: \\(a = [x_0 \\text{ repeated } L_0 \\text{ times}], [x_1 \\text{ repeated } L_1 \\text{ times}], \\dots\\)\n- **Self-similar-based**: \\(a\\) follows a power-law decay or rise \\(x_i = c \\cdot (i + 1)^{-\\alpha}\\)\n\nThe objective remains \\( \\frac{2\\cdot n\\cdot\\max_{0\\leq k\\leq 2n-1} \\text{conv}_{k}}{(\\sum a_{j})^2} \\).\n\nWe solve a reduced gradient problem under each active template with defined boundaries, and maintain a robust simplex projection keeping coefficients non-negative.\n\n## Implementation Procedure\n\n1. **Setup**\n   - Parse time parameters and initialize random seed\n   - Define target sequence lengths and objective dimension\n\n2. **Template Initialization**\n   - Create parent sequence for each length using periodic, sub-interval, and self-similar initializations\n   - For each, compute initial \\(\\max(\\text{conv})\\) and sum\n\n3. **Template Optimization Cycle**\n   ```python\n   def optimize_using_template(initial_sequence, n_points, template, timeout):\n       \"\"\"Perform optimization using specific template type.\"\"\"\n       # Clone and organize sequence per template\n       a = initial_sequence.copy()\n       s = a.sum()\n       if s < 0.01: return None\n   \n       # Transform sequence into template-specific representation\n       # For periodic: scale heights periodically\n       # For sub-interval: add structure breaks at n_points//2 boundaries\n       # For self-similar: introduce power-law coefficients\n   \n       # Determine template-specific parameters\n       if template == \"periodic\":\n           # Transform to periodic variant\n           pass\n   \n       elif template == \"cluster\":\n           # Creates 4 distinct clusters: [start1, end1], [start2, end2], ...\n           pass\n   \n       # Perform constrained optimization using projected gradient\n       runtemplate_optimization(a, ...)\n   ```\n\n4. **Core Template Optimization**\n   ```python\n   def runtemplate_optimization(a, n_points, max_iterations=25, verbosity=5):\n       \"\"\"Run chosen template variant using cyclic refinement.\"\"\"\n       # Cycle-based\n       n_segments = min(4, n_points)  # Max critical segments\n       a_periodic_seg = project_simplex(a) / np.sum(a, ...)\n       # Sub-interval-based\n       a_subinterval = add_structure_breaks(a_periodic)\n       # Self-similar\n       a_selfsimilar_seg = apply_power_law_transform(a_subinterval)\n   \n       # Create cycle array\n       candidates = [\n           (\"periodic_\"+n_points, a_periodic),\n           (\"cluster_\"+n_points, a_subinterval),\n           (\"selfsimilar_trunc\", a_selfsimilar)\n       ]\n   \n       # Run optimized LP or gradient descent on the template-specific forms\n       for name, specific in candidates:\n           specific = normalize_sequence(specific)\n           obj_val = evaluate_sequence(specific)\n           if obj_val < current_best[name]: \n               update_evaluate(name)\n           # Add cyclic refinement penalties for stuck patterns...\n       ```\n\n5. **Search Functions Integration**\n   ```python\n   def project_simplex(v):\n       n = len(v)\n       sorted_v = np.sort(v)[::-1]\n       unadjusted_v = v - (1/(n+1))*(np.arange(n)-n//2)\n       # Create simplex projection using Lagrange multipliers\n       return np.maximum(sorted_v - (sorted_v[0]*(n+1)-1)/n, 0.0)[::-1]  # Complex adjustment needed\n   ```\n\n6. **Fallback Handling**\n   ```python\n   def emergency_fallback(n, t_remaining):\n       \"\"\"Generate valid candidate if no progress.\"\"\"\n       base = np.ones(n)/n\n       while t_remaining > 3:\n           # Create truncated Gaussian profiles\n           pass\n       return base\n   ```\n\n7. **Termination Logic**\n   ```python\n   if max_iter > 100 and best_obj_improve_menth consecutivefails:\n        # Final random search with Gaussian niching\n        return None\n   else:\n        # Update findings and estimate timeout\n        pass\n   ```\n\n## Search Choices and Variations\n\nThe optimization will prioritize the following template variants:\n\n1. **Periodic Block Refinement** - Using `periodic_128`, `periodic_256`, `periodic_512`\n   - Expect smaller peaks from periodicity\n\n2. **Sub-interval Adaptive Splitting** - Initially 2 intervals, then 4, then finetune\n   - Allows sharp transitions between segments\n\n3. **Self-similar Power Law Sharpening** - Starting with uniform then optimizing \\(\\alpha\\) in \\(x_i = c \\cdot (i + 1)^{-\\alpha}\\)\n\nEach optimization variant has its own robustness estimate: periodic ones are robust to LP solvers if the segment count is not too large; sub-interval templates reduce complex coupling between coefficients, so faster convergence; self-similar templates guarantee non-negativity and convexity in objective.\n\n## Compute Budget and Robustness\n\n- **Estimated Time**: 1750 seconds (leaving 50 for system overhead)\n- **Memory Usage**: Each sub-interval template with length \\(n\\) uses \\(O(n)\\) storage for the array.\n- **First Stage Timeout**: 300 seconds for initializing and evaluating all templates\n- **Second Stage Timeout**: 700 seconds to search via perturbation and repair cycles\n- **Third Stage Timeout**: 800 seconds for final polishing\n\nWe implement safety checks against infinite loops by restarting periodic templates at a multiple of current iteration if improvement stalls.\n\n## Planning and Unitary Structure\n\nThe plan expects a sequence of length `n` in powers of two in:\n\n- **Periodic Templates**: 128, 256, 512 segments\n- **Sub-interval Templates**: Varied from 2 to 4 intervals\n- **Self-similar Templates**: Common structure\n\nEach template is checked for feasibility (non-negative values and nonlinear check).\n\nThe intention is to balance between structural search and repeated optimization cycles until improvement is detectable or timeout occurs.",
